course: dictionary
lesson: 599
title: Await: waiting for something
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: «Await» is a more formal word for «wait for». It means to wait for something, or to be ready and waiting for it. «We await your reply» is what a formal letter might say, and it means "we are waiting for your answer."
explain: Here is the key clue: «await» takes its object straight after it, with no «for». You say «await the bus», not «await for the bus». Compare «wait for the bus» with «await the bus». Both mean the same, but only one has «for».
explain: In stories, «awaits» often means something is ready and waiting for you. «Adventure awaits!» means that excitement is ready for you to find. In the sentence «Muslims believe the soul goes to God and awaits the Day of Judgement», awaits shows what a belief says will be waiting. Look for the clues around the word to be sure.
fact: Await means to wait for something, or to be ready and waiting for someone.
fact: «Await» is more formal than «wait for», so you will see it in letters, reports and stories more than in everyday talk.
fact: «Await» takes its object directly: «await your reply», not «await for your reply».
fact: «Eagerly awaited» means something people have been looking forward to with great excitement, like a new film.
fact: The forms are await, awaits, awaited and awaiting, as in «The results are awaited».
exercise: «We await your reply.» What does await mean here? | We are asking you to leave | *We are waiting for your answer | We are sure you are wrong | We are sending you a present
exercise: Which sentence uses await correctly? | We await for the results of the test. | We await to the results of the test. | *We await the results of the test. | We await at the results of the test.
exercise: «Adventure awaits!» What does this mean? | *Something exciting is ready and waiting for you | Adventure is very slow | Adventure is hiding from you | Adventure has gone away
test: Which word means about the same as await in «We await your reply»? | Hide | *Wait for | Ignore | Forget
test: Why do we say «await the bus» and not «await for the bus»? | *Because await takes its object directly, with no for | Because for is a rude word | Because the bus is too far away | Because await only works in stories
exam: Choose the sentence that uses await correctly. | We await to your call. | We await for your call. | We awaiting call your. | *We await your call.
